# {{senseid|en|road}} {{lb|en|historical}} The [[right]] or [[privilege]] of taking the side of the road near the wall when encountering another [[pedestrian]]; {{n-g|said to be taken or given}}.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1791|author=w:James Boswell|title=Life of Johnson|passage=He related to me the following minute anecdote of this period: 'In the last age, when my mother lived in London, there were two sets of people, those who gave the '''wall''', and those who took it; the peaceable and the quarrelsome. {{...}} Now it is fixed that every man keeps to the right; or, if one is taking the '''wall''', another yields it; and it is never a dispute.'}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1822|title=The Pamphleteer|page=118
|passage=All persons, in walking the streets, whose right sides are next the wall, are intitled to take the '''wall'''.}}
