====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[move]] on the [[feet]] by [[alternately]] [[setting]] each [[foot]] (or pair or group of feet: in the case of some non-human [[animals]] with four or more feet) [[forward]], with at least one foot on the [[ground]] at all times. Compare '''[[run]]'''.
#: {{ux|en|To '''walk''' briskly for an hour every day is to keep fit.}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|chapter=Prologue|page=16|passage=Athelstan Arundel walked home all the way, foaming and raging. {{...}} His mother lived at Pembridge Square, which is four good measured miles from Lincoln's Inn. He walked the whole way, '''walking''' through crowds, and under the noses of dray-horses, carriage-horses, and cart-horses, without taking the least notice of them.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=XXXIII|page=257|passage=Edward Churchill still attended to his work in a hopeless mechanical manner like a sleep-walker who '''walks''' safely on a well-known round. But his Roman collar galled him, his cossack stifled him, his biretta was as uncomfortable as a merry-andrew's cap and bells.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial|legal}} To "[[walk free]]", i.e. to win, or avoid, a criminal court case, particularly when [[actually]] [[guilty]].
#: {{ux|en|If you can’t present a better case, that robber is going to '''walk'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial|euphemistic}} Of an object, to go [[missing]] or be [[stolen]].
#: {{ux|en|If you leave your wallet lying around, it’s going to '''walk'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|cricket|of a batsman}} To walk off the field, as if [[given]] [[out]], after the [[fielding]] [[side]] [[appeal]]s and before the [[umpire]] has [[ruled]]; done as a matter of [[sportsmanship]] when the batsman believes he is out.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To travel (a distance) by walking.
#: {{ux|en|I '''walk''' two miles to school every day.}}
#: {{ux|en|The museum’s not far from here &ndash; you can '''walk''' it.}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|chapter=Prologue|page=16|passage=Athelstan Arundel '''walked''' home all the way, foaming and raging. {{...}} His mother lived at Pembridge Square, which is four good measured miles from Lincoln's Inn. He '''walked''' the whole way, walking through crowds, and under the noses of dray-horses, carriage-horses, and cart-horses, without taking the least notice of them.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To take for a walk or accompany on a walk.
#: {{ux|en|I '''walk''' the dog every morning.}}
#: {{ux|en|Will you '''walk''' me home?}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|act=II|scene=ii|page=47|passage=I will rather trust{{...}}a thief to '''walk''' my ambling gelding.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|with ''in''}} To appear in a [[parade]].
#: {{ux|en|Those who wish to '''walk''' in the parade this fall should sign up now.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|baseball}} To allow a batter to reach base by [[pitch]]ing four [[ball|balls]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|baseball|of a|batter}} To reach base by being pitched four balls.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of an object or machine, to move by [[shifting]] between two positions, as if it were walking.
#: {{ux|en|If we don't bolt the washing machine down, it's going to '''walk''' across the room.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause something to move in such a way.
#: {{ux|en|I carefully '''walked''' the ladder along the wall.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[full]]; to [[beat]] (cloth) to give it the consistency of [[felt]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[traverse]] by walking (or analogous gradual movement).
#: {{ux|en|I '''walked''' the streets aimlessly.}}
#: {{ux|en|Debugging this computer program involved '''walking''' the heap.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|aviation}} To operate the left and right [[throttle]]s of (an aircraft) in alternation.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|journal=Flying Magazine|volume=46|issue=3|page=18| passage=Still keeping his tail in the air, Red coaxed the “Airknocker” ahead and as we grasped his struts he slowly retarded the throttle. We '''walked''' the plane between two tiedown blocks and not until we had tied the struts did Red cut the switch.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial}} To leave, resign.
#: {{ux|en|If we don't offer him more money he'll '''walk'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Ireland|page=39|passage=He will make their cowes and garrans to '''walk'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To push (a vehicle) alongside oneself as one walks.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|author=John Forester|title=Bicycle Transportation: A Handbook for Cycling Transportation Engineers| pageurl= http://books.google.com/books?id=Qz4kAulpimgC&pg=PA245&dq=%22walked+his+bicycle%22| page=245| publisher=MIT Press| passage=The county had a successful defense only because the judge kept telling the jury at every chance that the cyclist should have '''walked''' his bicycle like a pedestrian.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To behave; to pursue a course of life; to conduct oneself.
#* {{RQ:Taylor Holy Living}}, [https://books.google.com/books?id=xIVmAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA35 page 35]
#*: We '''walk''' perversely with God, and he will '''walk''' crookedly toward us.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To go restlessly about; said of things or persons expected to remain quiet, such as a sleeping person, or the spirit of a dead person.
#* '''October 9, 1550''', {{w|Hugh Latimer}}, ''sermon preached at Stamford'', [https://books.google.com/books?id=R-pCAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA94 link]
#*: I heard a pen '''walking''' in the chimney behind the cloth.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=Rollo Ahmed|title=The Black Art|publisher=Long| location=London| page=32| passage=There have been reports of cases where, in the event of a girl having died, a man was chosen to go through the marriage ceremony and even have intercourse with the body, before burial, so that she might not "'''walk'''".}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To be in motion; to act; to move.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1590|book=II|canto=IV|stanza=5|page=233|passage=her toung did '''walke''' / In fowle reproch.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=III|scene=iii|page=288|passage=I have heard, but not believed, the spirits of the dead / May '''walk''' again.}}
#* {{RQ:Jonson Catiline|page=56|act=III|passage=Come, doe you thinke, I'ld '''walke''' in any plot}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|historical}} To put, keep, or train (a [[puppy]]) in a walk, or training area for [[dogfighting]].
# {{lb|en|informal|transitive}} To move (a guest) to another [[hotel]] if their [[confirmed]] [[reservation]] is not [[available]] on the day of [[check-in]].
# {{senseid|en|of a tool, to wander}} {{lb|en|machining|intransitive|of a tool, such as a [[drill bit]] or [[reamer]]}} To tend to move radially while feeding axially, whether tending toward on-center or tending toward off-center. {{ng|Walking may be desirable (e.g., when a reamer walks into [[concentricity]]) or undesirable (e.g., when a [[twist drill]] walks into [[eccentricity]].)}}
# {{lb|en|paintball}} To pull (a trigger) rapid-fire by alternating two fingers.

