===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|walc}}, {{m|enm|walk}}, {{m|enm|walke}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*wealc}} (as in {{cog|ang|wealcspinl}}) and {{m|ang|ġewealc|t=rolling, turning; motion, surging}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*walką}}.
Cognate with {{cog|is|volk|t=difficulty, hardship, trouble}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A trip made by walking.
#: {{ux|en|I take a '''walk''' every morning.}}
#: {{co|en|go on a '''walk'''}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|year=1894
|title=From Month to Month
|url=https://archive.org/details/chronicleoflond1894lond_7/
|journal=The Chronicle of the {{w|London Missionary Society}}
|issue=33
|publisher=Readers Union
|oclc=183334441
|page=220
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/chronicleoflond1894lond_7/page/220/
|text=FOUR BAPTISMS IN YING-SHAN<br>We had looked forward to four or five days' work in Ying-shan similar to that in Yün-mung,but at the end of our two days' '''walk''' from the one city to the other (they lie more than fifty miles apart), Mr. Terrell had a touch of fever, so we judged it best to remain in Ying-shan only for a day and then travel as quickly as possible by chair to Teh-ngan to consult our good friend, Dr. Morley, of the Wesleyan Mission Hospital in that city, and from thence take boat for Hankow.}}
# A distance walked.
#: {{ux|en|It’s a long '''walk''' from my house to the library.}}
#: {{ux|en|The precinct is about ten minutes’ '''walk''', [[straight ahead]], so you can’t miss it.}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} An Olympic Games track event requiring that the heel of the leading foot touch the ground before the toe of the trailing foot leaves the ground.
# A manner of walking; a person's style of walking.
#: {{ux|en|The Ministry of Silly '''Walks''' is underfunded this year.}}
# A path, [[sidewalk]]/[[pavement]] or other maintained place on which to walk.
#: {{cot|en|trail}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=John Buchan|title=The Outgoing of the Tide
|passage=And then it appeared to the young man that he was walking his love up the grass '''walk''' of Heriotside, with the house close by him.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A person's [[conduct]] or [[course]] in [[life]].
#: {{uxi|en|people of different ages and '''walks'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=Sir Thomas Henry Hall Caine; Sir Hall Caine; John Parker Anderson|title=Life of Samuel Taylor Coleridge|page=136
|passage=Men like Stuart who had no desire to extol Coleridge's virtues, and other witnesses quite as hostile, to whom a moral dereliction could hardly be a mortal offence, were loud in praise of the purity of his '''walk''' in life.}}
# {{lb|en|poker}} A situation where all players [[fold]] to the [[big blind]], as their first [[action]] (instead of [[call]]ing or [[raise|raising]]), once they get their [[card]]s.
# {{senseid|en|baseball}} {{lb|en|baseball}} An award of first base to a batter following four balls being thrown by the pitcher; known in the rules as a "base on balls".
#: {{ux|en|The pitcher now has two '''walks''' in this inning alone.}}
# In [[coffee]], [[coconut]], and other [[plantation]]s, the space between them. <!--the plants? def is from W1913.-->
# {{lb|en|Caribbean|Belize|Guyana|Jamaica}} An area of an [[estate]] planted with fruit-bearing trees.<ref>Lise Winer (ed.), ''Dictionary of the English/Creole of Trinidad and Tobago'', McGill-Queen’s University Press, 2008, p.{{nbsp}}940.<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=_n82hsbDJBMC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup></ref>
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1755|author=William Belgrove|title=A Treatise upon Husbandry or Planting|location=Boston|page=14|url=https://archive.org/details/treatiseuponhusb00belg
|passage=Twenty Acres of Land well kept in a Plantain '''Walk''', will afford a very considerable Support, as Plantains are as hearty a Food as [[eddoe|Eddoes]], and the Plantain '''Walk''' may be a Nursery for declining Slaves, as well as to fatten old Cattle when they are past Labour.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1803|author=w:Robert Charles Dallas|title=The History of the Maroons|location=London|publisher=Longman and Rees|section=Volume 1, Letter 4, [https://archive.org/details/cihm_44228/page/n224/mode/1up page 80]
|passage=For half a mile from Vaughansfield the road, now a mere track, leads through pastures and a coffee-'''walk''' to the foot of a very steep hill {{...}}}}
#* '''1961''', {{w|Wilson Harris}}, ''The Far Journey of Oudin'', Book 2, Chapter 6, in ''The Guyana Quartet'', London: Faber and Faber, 1985, p.{{nbsp}}150,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/guyanaquartet00harr]</sup>
#*: One day he knew he would build this identical palace for himself. Not next to the road like now—where the present cottage was—but half a mile inside the coconut '''walk'''.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=w:Olive Senior|chapter=Window|title=Discerner of Hearts|location=Toronto|publisher=McClelland & Stewart|page=66|url=https://archive.org/details/discernerofheart00oliv
|passage=He couldn’t sleep and took to walking outside at night, to look at the stars, to feel the cool air, and for a long time wasn’t even conscious that he always ended up standing in the darkness of the cocoa '''walk''' staring at the shutters of Bridget’s room.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} A place for keeping and training [[puppy|puppies]] for [[dogfighting]].
# {{lb|en|historical}} An [[enclosed]] [[area]] in which a [[gamecock]] is [[confine]]d to prepare him for fighting.
# {{lb|en|graph theory}} A [[sequence]] of alternating [[vertex|vertices]] and [[edge|edges]], where each edge's endpoints are the preceding and following vertices in the sequence. Compare '''[[path]]''', '''[[trail]]'''.
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} Something very easily accomplished; a [[walk in the park]].
#* '''1980''', Robert Barr, ''The Coming Out Present'' (episode of ''Detective'', BBC radio drama; around 16 min 20 sec)
#*: And for the strongroom itself, he can tell us where to find the combination of the day. We had allowed four hours, Joe, but with this help, once you get us inside, it's a '''walk'''! I've been timing it.
# {{lb|en|UK|finance|slang|dated}} A [[cheque]] drawn on a [[bank]] that was not a member of the London Clearing and whose [[sort code]] was allocated on a one-off basis; they had to be "walked" (hand-delivered by messengers).

