##* {{quote-book|en|year=1518|chapter=Abbot of Peterborough ''v.'' Power and others|editor=[[d:Q18912326|Isaac Saunders Leadam]]|title=Select Cases before the King’s Council in the Star Chamber, commonly called The Court of Star Chamber|volume_plain=volume II: A.D. 1509–1544|series={{w|Selden Society}}|location={{w|London}}|publisher={{w|Bernard Quaritch}}|year_published=1911|page=127|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/selectcasesbefor0025engl/page/127/mode/1up|text={{...}} within v '''wardes''' of the same Towne which byn conteyned in seuerall partes in the same Towne ther hath byn v Cunstables that is to say in euery '''ward''' oon for the conseruacion of the kynges Peace and other good Rule {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1588|author=W. Smith|title=Brief Descr. Lond. {{noitalic|(MS. Harl. 6363)}}|page_plain=leaf 13|text=There is also The Wardmote Enquest, Chosen euery St. Thomas day, in euery '''ward''' a quest.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1590|author=w:Anthony Munday;w:Henry Chettle|editors=w:Thomas Heywood;[[w:Thomas Dekker (writer)|Thomas Dekker]];w:William Shakespeare|title=[[w:Sir Thomas More (play)|Sir Thomas More]]|section=Act II, scene iv|line=226|text=We meete at the Guildehall and there determine That thorow euery '''warde''' the watche be clad In armour.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1598–1603|author=John Stow|authorlink=John Stow|editor=w:Charles Lethbridge Kingsford|title=A Survey of London {{...}}: Reprinted from the text of 1603|volume=I|location=w:Oxford|publisher=[[w:Oxford University Press#Clarendon Press|At the Clarendon Press]]|year_published=1908|page=117|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/cu31924031041589/page/n228/mode/1up|text={{smc|The}} Auncient diuision of this Cittie, was into '''Wardes''' or Aldermanries: and therefore I will beginne at the East, and so proceede thorough the high and most principall streete of the cittie to the west after this manner.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1603|author=William Shakespeare|authorlink=William Shakespeare|title=w:Measure for Measure|section=act II, scene i|line=281|text=They do you wrong to put you so oft vpon ’t [''sc.'' the office of constable]. Are there not men in your '''Ward''' sufficient to serue it?}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1631|author=Gouge|title=God’s Arrows|section={{smc|v.}} xix. 432|text=London should have as many Artillery Gardens, as it hath '''Wards'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|8|passage=Throughout the trembling city placed a guard,<br>Dealing an equal share to every '''ward'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1700|author=Evelyn|title=Diary|section=6 May 1645|text=Rome…is divided into 14 Regions or '''Wards'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1715|author=Leoni|title=Palladio’s Archit.|volume=II|year_published=1742|page=72|text=In the '''Ward''' [It. ''regione''] of the Temple of Peace, stood a Colossus.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1733|author=Swift|title=On Poetry|page=286|text=In ev’ry Street a City-bard Rules, like an Alderman his '''Ward'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1751|chapter=St. Albans|title=Engl. Gazetteer|volume=I|text=There are four '''wards''' here, in each of which are a constable, and two church-wardens.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1824|author=G. Chalmers|title=Caledonia|volume=III|section=chapter vi|page=569|text=By an act of Parliament, in 1800, for regulating the police of Glasgow, that city was divided into '''wards'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1854|author=Lowell|chapter_plain=''Camb. 30 Yrs. Ago''|title=Pr. Wks.|volume=I|year_published=1890|page=94|text=I would rather have had that slow, conscientious vote of P.’s alone, than to have been chosen Alderman of the '''Ward''' !}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1863|author=Cox|title=Instit.|section={{smc|iii.}} ix. 730|text=Large boroughs are divided into '''wards''', which elect their councillors severally.}}
