===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|warden}}, from {{inh|en|ang|weardian||to watch, guard, keep, protect, preserve; hold, possess, occupy, inhabit; rule, govern}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wardēn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wardōną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*wardāną||to guard}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wer-||to heed, defend}}. {{doublet|en|guard}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To keep in [[safety]], to [[watch]] over, to [[guard]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=VIII|stanza=3|passage=Whoſe gates he found faſt ſhut, ne liuing wight<br>To '''ward''' the ſame, nor anſwere commers call}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[defend]], to [[protect]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus|act=III|scene=i|passage=Tell him it was a hand that '''warded''' him<br>From a thousand dangers.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1603|translator=John Florio|author=Michel de Montaigne|title=Essays|section=II.3
|passage=they went to ſeeke their owne death, and ruſhed amidſt the thickeſt of their enemies, with an intention, rather to ſtrike, than to '''ward''' themſelves.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[fend off]], to [[repel]], to [[turn aside]], as anything mischievous that approaches. {{q|usually followed by {{l|en|off}}}}
#: {{syn|en|ward off}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=ii|page=26|passage=Draw forth thy ſword, thou mightie man at armes,<br>Intending but to raiſe my charmed ſkin:<br>And ''Ioue'' himſelfe will ſtretch his hand from heauen,<br>To '''ward''' the blow, and ſhield me ſafe from harme, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1609|author=w:Samuel Daniel|title=The Civile Wares
|passage=Now '''wards''' a felling blow, now strikes again.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1717|author=w:Joseph Addison|title=Metamorphoses
|passage=The pointed javelin '''warded''' off his rage.}}
#* {{RQ:Watts Improvement
|passage=It instructs the scholar in the various methods of '''warding''' off the force of objections.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[vigilant]]; to keep guard.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=VIII|passage=They for vs fight, they watch and dewly '''ward''',<br>And their bright Squadrons round about vs plant {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2017-11-16|author=w:Jo Ellison|title=Help: the gym has turned us into slobs|journal=w:Financial Times|url=https://www.ft.com/content/1ae28e48-c9f6-11e7-ab18-7a9fb7d6163e|passage=Having slapped into middle age, where thoughts of one’s general infallibility are tempered by the realisation that those creaky, achy complaints are signs of certain decrepitude, I have decided to '''ward''' against further gravitational decline by hauling my saggy, sorry self to the gym.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To act on the [[defensive]] with a weapon.

