===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|waven}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wafian||to wave, fluctuate, waver in mind, wonder}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wabōn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wabōną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*wabjaną||to wander, sway}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*webʰ-||to move to and from, wander}}.

Cognate with {{cog|gmh|waben||to wave}}, {{cog|de|wabern||to waft}}, {{cog|is|váfa||to fluctuate, waver, doubt}}. See also {{l|en|waver}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move [[back]] and [[forth]] [[repeatedly]] and somewhat loosely.
#: {{ux|en|The flag '''waved''' in the gentle breeze.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 1, 2011|author=Tom Fordyce|work=BBC Sport| title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/rugby_union/15124512.stm Rugby World Cup 2011: England 16-12 Scotland]| passage=But the World Cup winning veteran's left boot was awry again, the attempt sliced horribly wide of the left upright, and the saltires were '''waving''' aloft again a moment later when a long pass in the England midfield was picked off to almost offer up a breakaway try.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move one's [[hand]] back and forth (generally above the shoulders) in greeting or departure.
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1978
|author=w:Richard Nixon
|title=RN: the Memoirs of Richard Nixon
|url=https://archive.org/details/rnmemoirsofricha00nixo/
|publisher=Grosset & Dunlap
|ISBN=0-448-14374-7
|LCCN=77-87793
|OCLC=760525066
|OL=7561812M
|page=1090
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/rnmemoirsofricha00nixo/page/1090/
|text=I raised my arms in a final salute. I smiled. I '''waved''' goodbye. I turned into the helicopter, the door was closed, the red carpet was rolled up.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|metonymic}} To call attention to, or give a direction or command to, by a waving motion, as of the hand; to signify by waving; to beckon; to signal; to indicate.
#: {{ux|en|I '''waved''' goodbye from across the room.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|act=I|scene=iv|passage=Look, with what courteous action / It '''waves''' you to a more removed ground.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Princess|2
|passage=She spoke, and bowing '''waved''' / Dismissal.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have an [[undulating]] or [[wavy]] form.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To raise into inequalities of surface; to give an undulating form or surface to.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|act=IV|scene=vi|passage=horns whelked and '''waved''' like the enridged sea}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[style]] (the [[hair]]) so as to produce a wavy texture.
#* {{RQ:Christie Autobiography|II|4|passage=There was also hairdressing: hairdressing, too, really was hairdressing in those times — no running a comb through it and that was that. It was curled, frizzed, '''waved''', put in curlers overnight, '''waved''' with hot tongs;{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|baseball}} To swing and miss at a pitch.
#: {{ux|en|Jones '''waves''' at strike one.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cause]] to move back and forth repeatedly.
#: {{ux|en|The starter '''waved''' the flag to begin the race.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=13 Aug 77|volume=5|number=6|journal=Gay Community News|page=9|author=Pala Bennett|author2=Mary Jo Risher|title="I'm A Mother, And I'm A Good One."|text=His father has '''waved''' bills in front of face and said to him — see what you've cost me.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|metonymic}} To [[signal]] (someone or something) with a waving movement.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To fluctuate; to waver; to be in an unsettled state.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|act=II|scene=ii|passage=He '''waved''' indifferently 'twixt doing them neither good nor harm.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|ergative}} To move like a wave, or by floating; to [[waft]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Anecdotes of Mr. Hogarth: And Explanatory Descriptions of the Plates of Hogarth Restored| page=137| author=w:William Hogarth| year=1803| passage=But in the last, this dotted line, by the twisting as well as the bending of the horn, is changed from the '''waving''' into the serpentine line}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Robin Hood and Little John: or, The merry men of Sherwood forest| author=Pierce Egan| year=1850| page=272| passage=the flowers will not bloom less brightly, nor the grass be less green and fresh because it is waving over the head of one who loved to look upon their tender beauty while living.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Rambles in Search of Ferns| page=31| year=1851| author=Margaret Plues| passage=The cypresslike ferns were not '''waving''' over these, as they '''waved''' over the corals in the wood, but the little spleenwort, called Wall-rue, was resolved that their tomb should not be without verdure.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Bound to the Wheel| page=89| author=John Saunders| year=1866| passage=The moonlight fell into the room, and the shadows '''waved''' over him}}
