===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*weǵ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|waiten}}, from {{der|en|xno|waiter}}, {{m|fro|waitier}} (compare {{cog|fr|guetter}} from {{cog|fro|gaitier}}, {{m|fro|guaitier}}), from {{der|en|frk|*wahtwēn|t=to watch, guard}}, derivative of {{der|en|frk|*wahtu|t=guard, watch}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*wahtwō|t=guard, watch}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*weǵ-|t=to be fresh, cheerful, awake}}. 

Cognate with {{cog|goh|wahtēn||to watch, guard}}, {{cog|nds-de|wachten||to wait}}, {{cog|nl|wachten||to wait, expect}}, {{cog|fr|guetter||to watch out for}}, {{cog|stq|wachtje||to wait}}, {{cog|fy|wachtsje|t=to wait}}, {{cog|frr|wachtjen||to stand, stay put}}. More at {{l|en|watch}}.

In some senses, merged or influenced by {{der|en|enm|waiten}}, {{m|enm|weiten|t=to do good to, lie in wait for, to contrive good or harm on, catch, snare}}, from {{der|en|non|veita|t=to give help to, assist, grant, cause to happen}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*waitijaną|t=to show, guide, advise, direct}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*weyd-|t=to see, know}}.

Largely overtook native {{ncog|enm|biden}}, from {{ncog|ang|bīdan}}, source of {{m|en|bide}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[delay]] [[movement]] or [[action]] until some event or time; to [[remain]] neglected or in readiness.
#: {{ux|en|'''Wait''' here until your car arrives.}}
#: {{ux|en|I’m still '''waiting''' for you to pay me back the money I lent you..}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Poems 1673|When I Consider|page=59|pageref=n74|passage=They also serve who only stand and '''wait'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Aeneis
|passage=Haste, my dear father; 'tis no time to '''wait'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=IV|page=46|passage=No matter how early I came down, I would find him on the veranda, smoking cigarettes, or otherwise his man would be there with a message to say that his master would shortly join me if I would kindly '''wait'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1948|month=March and April|title=Notes and News: London Transport Plans|journal=Railway Magazine|page=132|text=The South London tramway replacement will have to '''wait''', possibly five years, because of the slowing down of bus manufacture due to national requirements.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|stative|US}} To [[wait tables]]; to serve customers in a [[restaurant]] or other [[eating]] [[establishment]].
#: {{ux|en|She used to '''wait''' in this joint.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|now|rare}} To delay movement or action until the arrival or occurrence of; to [[await]]. (Now generally superseded by “[[wait for]]”.)
#: {{coi|en|to '''wait''' one’s turn}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Aeneis
|passage=Awed with these words, in camps they still abide, / And '''wait''' with longing looks their promised guide.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=w:Hilary Mantel|title=A Place of Greater Safety|publisher=Harper Perennial|year_published=2007|page=30
|passage=The Court had assembled, to '''wait''' events, in the huge antechamber known as the Œil de Boeuf.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[attend]] on; to [[accompany]]; especially, to attend with ceremony or respect.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Aeneis
|passage=He chose a thousand horse, the flower of all / His warlike troops, to '''wait''' the funeral.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1714|author=w:Nicholas Rowe|title=The Tragedy of Jane Shore
|passage=Remorse and heaviness of heart shall '''wait''' thee, / And everlasting anguish be thy portion.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To attend as a consequence; to [[follow]] upon; to [[accompany]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|colloquial}} To [[defer]] or [[postpone]] (especially a meal).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1791|author={{w|Charlotte Smith (writer)|Charlotte Smith}}|title=Celestina|publisher=Broadview|year_published=2004|page=185
|passage=Montague Thorold, who impatiently watched her wherever she went, came to tell her that his mother '''waited''' breakfast for her.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|except in phrases}} To watch with [[malicious]] intent; to [[lie in wait]]
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To remain [[faithful]] to one’s [[partner]] or [[betrothed]] during a [[prolonged]] period of absence.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1957|author=Dagny Taggart; Francisco d'Anconia, Ayn Rand's|title=[[w:Atlas_Shrugged|Atlas Shrugged]]
|passage=She did not question him. Before leaving, she asked only, "When will I see you again?" He answered, "I don't know. Don't '''wait''' for me, Dagny. Next time we meet, you will not want to see me."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1974|author=w:The Bee Gees|title=w:Night Fever
|passage=''I will '''wait''' / Even if it takes forever / I will wait / Even if it takes a lifetime''}}

