===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|whirlewind}}, {{m|enm|whirlewynde}}, equivalent to {{com|en|whirl|wind}}. Compare {{cog|dum|wervelwint}}, {{cog|non|hvirfilvindr}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈwɜːlwɪnd/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈ(h)wɝlwɪnd/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-whirlwind.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[windstorm]] of limited extent, such as a [[tornado]], [[dust devil]], or [[waterspout]], characterized by an inward [[spiral]] motion of the air with an upward [[current]] in the center; a [[vortex]] of air.
#* {{RQ:More Divine Dialogues|edition=2nd|para=XXXVIII|page=515|passage=VVhile in the mean time there iſſued out on the Eaſt-ſide a ſtrong VVind, but pure and refreſhing, vvhich dividing into ſeveral parts that turned round became ſo many innocuous '''VVhirl-vvinds''' of ſincere Air, tinctured only vvith a cool refreſhing ſmell, as if it had paſſed over ſome large field of Lilies and Roſes.}}
#* {{RQ:Shelley Revolt of Islam|passage=And his hoofs ground the rocks to fire and dust, / His strong sides made the torrents rise in spray, / And turbulence, as if a '''whirlwind'''’s gust / Surrounded us; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A person or body of objects or [[events]] sweeping violently onward.
#: {{syn|en|vortex|maelstrom}}
#: {{ux|en|Once he got that new scooter he turned into a '''whirlwind''' and damaged all the flowers.}}
#: {{ux|en|The weeks leading up to the convention were a '''whirlwind''' of preparation and hurried activity.}}
#: {{ux|en|[[swept up]] in the '''whirlwind''' of war}}

